WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:11.680
The Kalbuco volcano erupted in April 2015, spewing a plume of ash and sulfur dioxide

00:11.680 --> 00:14.880
over 10 miles into the air.

00:14.880 --> 00:19.040
Volcanic ash like this can cause airplane engines to fail, and it can persist in the

00:19.040 --> 00:21.080
atmosphere for a long time.

00:21.080 --> 00:27.120
It can be difficult to distinguish the volcanic plume from ordinary clouds, but NASA scientists

00:27.120 --> 00:31.680
are developing a new way to map the full three-dimensional structure of a volcanic

00:31.680 --> 00:38.560
cloud, providing improved information for air traffic management.

00:38.560 --> 00:45.360
The concentration of sulfur dioxide is mapped by the SUMI MPP satellite as it passes overhead,

00:45.360 --> 00:52.600
and then looks back and also measures the vertical profile of the cloud in three separate slices.

00:52.600 --> 00:57.440
The location and height of the particles, as well as the amount of sulfur dioxide, is

00:57.440 --> 01:05.080
being integrated into models of weather patterns to forecast the spread of the volcanic cloud.

01:05.080 --> 01:10.480
The high resolution of the data from the vertical profiles allows a more accurate forecast in

01:10.480 --> 01:15.840
the days, weeks, and months after an eruption, which could reduce airline cancellations and

01:15.840 --> 01:18.040
rerouting costs.

01:18.040 --> 01:23.240
The final product will be available in near real time for use by operational agencies

01:23.240 --> 01:25.040
making decisions about flight safety.

